<!-- Each <lastmod> is the date that page's own file last changed, not the date
     this file was last touched. Derive it per page rather than stamping today
     across the board: a sitemap where every entry carries the same date is a
     known low-quality signal, and crawlers discount it, which costs freshness
     credit on the pages that genuinely did change.

     To recheck one entry, ask git for the committer date of that page's most
     recent commit (git log, limited to the one path, formatted as %cs). Check
     before committing, so a page edited in the working tree gets the date of
     the commit it is about to land in rather than its previous one.

     The 2026-08-26 dates below are all the same for a legitimate reason rather
     than a lazy one: that change set wired path_helpers.js into every page and
     consolidated the JSON-LD author nodes, so it edited all five, and the
     procedure above genuinely returns one date for each. Do not read the
     matching dates as licence to stamp today across the board next time.

     These dates track when the file changed. The dateModified values in each
     page's JSON-LD track when its content changed, so the two drift apart
     whenever a page is edited without its prose moving. That drift is expected;
     do not "fix" it by copying one field into the other. -->
